    "content": {
      "question_html": "<div class=\"q-line\"><div class=\"q-line__text\">Describe how the body controls blood glucose concentration after a meal and during a long period without food.</div><span class=\"q-marks\">(6)</span></div>",
      "question_image_url": null,
      "mark_scheme_html": "<p>Marked by level of response: 5–6 marks for a detailed, logically sequenced account of both situations.</p>\n<p>Indicative content:</p>\n<ul><li>After a meal the blood glucose concentration rises, which is detected by the pancreas</li><li>The pancreas releases insulin</li><li>Insulin causes glucose to move from the blood into cells; the liver and muscles convert glucose to glycogen</li><li>Without food the blood glucose concentration falls and the pancreas releases glucagon</li><li>Glucagon causes the liver to convert glycogen back to glucose</li><li>This is an example of negative feedback</li></ul>",
      "mark_scheme_image_url": null
    }
  }
